Start by drawing a digit. This complete program shows 5 and waits for a keypad key before returning.

1. Press Ctrl+P > CHIP-8 / assembly. Press F3 New and name it FIRST.
2. Type the program below, one instruction per line. Press F1 Save, then F5 Back.
3. With FIRST.asm selected, choose F4 Actions > Compatibility profile > SUPER-CHIP modern.
4. Press F2 Run. This profile supports the EXIT instruction at the end.

Complete program
CLS
LD V0, 5
LD F, V0
LD V1, 10
LD V2, 8
DRW V1, V2, 5
LD V3, K
EXIT

You should see 5 near the upper-left of the CHIP-8 display. Press and release X to return.Begin with Run your first CHIP-8 program. Its picture and key wait make the result visible. Instruction reference pages show smaller fragments so you can see one operation at a time.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 7
ADD V0, 2

In this fragment V0 becomes 9. It does not display a number and is not a complete ROM. Add fragments to a complete program before running them.Keep the .asm file: it is the text you can edit. Running assembles a separate .ch8 ROM. Opening a .ch8 file does not turn it back into editable assembly.

1. Create an assembly file named SAVETEST and enter the program.
2. Press F1 Save, then F5 Back.
3. Select SAVETEST.asm and press F1 Open. F2 saves and builds/runs it.

Complete program
CLS
LD V0, 5
LD F, V0
LD V1, 10
LD V2, 8
DRW V1, V2, 5
LD V3, K
EXIT

After reopening, the same source is there. Running it in SUPER-CHIP modern displays 5 and waits for a keypad key.Use V0 through VF to keep small numbers. Each holds 0 to 255; arithmetic wraps around when it goes past that range. VF also carries arithmetic and drawing flags. I holds a memory address.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 0x0A
ADD V0, 2

V0 becomes 12.

This instruction fragment changes registers; it does not print their values. Use Run your first CHIP-8 program for a complete visible example.Give a jump target a label ending in a colon. Keep its spelling the same wherever you use it. Add a semicolon comment to explain a line. Instructions ignore case; labels do not.

Instruction fragment
JP start
start:
LD V0, 7 ; seven

Jumps to start and sets V0 to 7.Point I at the built-in font with LD F,V0. The next DRW reads five rows of that glyph. Change the first number below to choose another hexadecimal digit, from 0 to 15.

1. Create or open an .asm file and select SUPER-CHIP modern.
2. Type the example and press F2 Save & run.

Complete program
CLS
LD V0, 9
LD F, V0
LD V1, 10
LD V2, 8
DRW V1, V2, 5
LD V3, K
EXIT

This complete SUPER-CHIP program displays 9. Press and release a keypad key to return.LD F,Vx chooses the small font glyph for a hexadecimal digit. For a decimal number with several digits, LD B,Vx writes its hundreds, tens and ones to three memory bytes. The fragment below splits 123 into 1, 2 and 3.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 123
LD I, 0x300
LD B, V0

Memory 0x300..0x302 contains 1, 2, 3.CHIP-8 expects sixteen hexadecimal keys. These PicoCalc keys provide them:

1 2 3 4   =   1 2 3 C
Q W E R   =   4 5 6 D
A S D F   =   7 8 9 E
Z X C V   =   A 0 B F

LD Vx,K waits for a press and release. Esc stops a running program.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, K

Press and release X: V0 receives the CHIP-8 key value 0. This fragment does not draw the value.Put a duration into ST to play a tone. The sound and delay timers count down at 60 ticks per second, so 30 ticks is about half a second. This complete program waits for the delay, then exits.

1. Use SUPER-CHIP modern for this program.
2. Save the source and run it with F2.

Complete program
LD V0, 30
LD DT, V0
LD ST, V0
wait:
LD V1, DT
SE V1, 0
JP wait
EXIT

In SCHIP, plays for roughly half a second, waits for the delay and exits.Add a byte or another register. ADD Vx,byte wraps without changing VF; ADD Vx,Vy sets VF when there is a carry. ADD I,Vx changes the address in I.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 250
LD V1, 10
ADD V0, V1

V0 becomes 4 and VF becomes 1.Keep only the bits set in both registers. The answer replaces Vx.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 0x0F
LD V1, 0x03
AND V0, V1

V0 becomes 3.Run a subroutine at a label, then use RET to come back. Calls can nest up to sixteen levels.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 3
CALL twice
JP done
twice:
ADD V0, V0
RET
done:
JP done

V0 becomes 6, then waits in the done loop.Clear the display. Call it before drawing a new picture.

Instruction fragment
CLS

The display is blank.Put bytes into the program, usually for a sprite. Jump over the data so the machine does not try to execute it. Pad an odd byte count before the next instruction.

Instruction fragment
JP start
image:
DB 0xF0, 0x90, 0xF0, 0
start:
LD I, image

Defines three image rows and one padding byte; I points to them.Draw sprite bytes from memory at I, using Vx and Vy as the position. Drawing uses XOR: set pixels toggle. VF reports a collision. Height 1 to 15 draws that many eight-pixel rows; Super-CHIP height 0 uses a 16x16 sprite. DRAW is another spelling.

Instruction fragment
CLS
LD V0, 10
LD V1, 8
LD I, dot
DRW V0, V1, 1
JP done
dot:
DB 0x80, 0
done:
JP done

Draws one pixel at logical (10,8). SCHIP low-resolution mode scales logical pixels on its 128x64 backing display.Put a 16-bit word into the program, high byte first. Prefer instruction names when writing code; words are useful when you need exact bytes.

Instruction fragment
DW 0x6007

Emits bytes 0x60,0x07: the opcode for LD V0,7.Stop the program and return to the calculator. Choose a Super-CHIP profile; classic CHIP-8 does not have this instruction.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 7
EXIT

Returns after setting V0 to 7.Restore V0 through Vx from the temporary Super-CHIP flags. Choose V0 through V7.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 42
FLAGSAVE V0
LD V0, 0
FLAGLOAD V0

V0 returns to 42.Keep V0 through Vx in the temporary Super-CHIP flag registers. Choose V0 through V7. These are not saved SD files.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 42
FLAGSAVE V0
LD V0, 0
FLAGLOAD V0

V0 returns to 42.Choose the Super-CHIP 128x64 display and clear it. Use a Super-CHIP profile.

Instruction fragment
HIGH
CLS

Selects the higher-resolution display.Jump to an address or label. A jump back to the same loop repeats until Esc stops the program.

Instruction fragment
loop:
LD V0, 5
JP loop

Repeatedly sets V0 to 5. Esc stops the program.Jump to the given address plus V0. Profile differences matter when you are adapting a ROM.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 2
JPOFF 0x204
LD V1, 1
LD V1, 9

Jumps to 0x206, skipping the instruction that sets V1 to 1.Copy a value into a register, or move a value to or from a special register. V0 through VF hold bytes; I holds an address.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 12
LD V1, V0

Both V0 and V1 contain 12.Read V0 through Vx from memory starting at I. LD Vx,[I] is another spelling. Reset I between a SAVE and LOAD for portable code.

Instruction fragment
LD I, 0x300
LD V0, 7
SAVE V0
LD I, 0x300
LD V0, 0
LOAD V0

V0 returns to 7.Choose the classic 64x32 logical display and clear it. This mode-switch instruction needs a Super-CHIP profile.

Instruction fragment
LOW
CLS

Selects the classic logical display.Set a bit when it is set in either register. The answer replaces Vx.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 0x0A
LD V1, 0x05
OR V0, V1

V0 becomes 15.Return to the instruction after the matching CALL. A RET without a call causes a return-stack error.

Instruction fragment
CALL twice
JP done
twice:
ADD V0, V0
RET
done:
JP done

Calls a subroutine, doubles V0 and returns. The final jump keeps it from falling into subroutine data.Generate a random byte, then keep only the bits allowed by the mask. A mask of 0x0F gives a value from 0 to 15.

Instruction fragment
RND V0, 0x0F

V0 is between 0 and 15.Store V0 through Vx in memory starting at I. This is CHIP-8 memory, not an SD save. Reset I explicitly before a later load if you want code to work across profiles.

Instruction fragment
LD I, 0x300
LD V0, 7
LD V1, 9
SAVE V1

Memory 0x300 contains 7 and 0x301 contains 9.Scroll down by a number from 0 to 15. Use a Super-CHIP profile. Modern and HP48 profiles scale low-resolution scrolling differently.

Instruction fragment
HIGH
SCD 2

Moves high-resolution pixels down two rows.Scroll the display left. In high resolution it moves four pixels; low-resolution behaviour depends on the Super-CHIP profile.

Instruction fragment
HIGH
SCL

Moves existing high-resolution pixels left by four.Scroll the display right. In high resolution it moves four pixels; low-resolution behaviour depends on the Super-CHIP profile.

Instruction fragment
HIGH
SCR

Moves existing high-resolution pixels right by four.Skip the next instruction when Vx equals the supplied byte or register. Otherwise continue normally.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 5
SE V0, 5
LD V1, 1
LD V2, 9

Skips LD V1,1; V2 becomes 9.Shift left one bit and put the high bit into VF. The two-register form depends on the profile: VIP reads Vy; the other profiles read Vx.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 0x81
SHL V0

V0 becomes 2 and VF becomes 1.Shift right one bit and put the bit shifted out into VF. With one register operand, that register is both the source and destination. The two-register form depends on the profile.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 9
SHR V0

V0 becomes 4 and VF becomes 1.Skip the next instruction if the hexadecimal key named by Vx is not held.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 1
SKNP V0
JP 0x202

Skips the jump when key 1 is released.Skip the next instruction if the hexadecimal key named by Vx is held. See Use the PicoCalc keypad for the physical keys.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 1
SKP V0
JP 0x202

Waits here while key 1 is not held; pressing 1 skips the jump.Skip the next instruction when Vx differs from the supplied byte or register. Otherwise continue normally.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 5
SNE V0, 6
LD V1, 1
LD V2, 9

Skips LD V1,1; V2 becomes 9.Subtract Vy from Vx. A result below zero wraps around; VF is 1 when no borrow was needed.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 9
LD V1, 4
SUB V0, V1

V0 becomes 5 and VF becomes 1.Subtract Vx from Vy and leave the answer in Vx. VF is 1 when no borrow was needed.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 3
LD V1, 8
SUBN V0, V1

V0 becomes 5 and VF becomes 1.Toggle the bits in Vx that are set in Vy. The answer replaces Vx.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 0x0F
LD V1, 0x03
XOR V0, V1

V0 becomes 12.Read the line number in the assembler message and check that line. For example, LD V0,256 is out of range: a byte must be 0 to 255. Correct it to the example below, then save and build again.

1. Return to the editor and use F4 Tools > Go to line if needed.
2. Fix the instruction, then press F2 Save & run.

Complete program
LD V0, 7
EXIT

In SUPER-CHIP modern the corrected program builds and returns. It changes V0 without drawing anything.Loading registers does not draw them. A visible picture needs I to point at sprite data and DRW to draw it. Check the selected profile as well. Try this complete program first.

Complete program
CLS
LD V0, 5
LD F, V0
LD V1, 10
LD V2, 8
DRW V1, V2, 5
LD V3, K
EXIT

You should see 5. If it appears, compare your own font/sprite address and draw instruction with this example. Press and release X to return.If a ROM draws or responds differently than expected, check its profile. From the assembly/ROM list, choose F4 Actions > Compatibility profile. Match the ROM first; changing its code should come later.

Instruction fragment
LD V0, 8
LD V1, 2
SHR V0, V1

VIP shifts V1 into V0, giving 1. SCHIP shifts V0, giving 4. VF receives the bit shifted out.

CHIP-8 (VIP), CHIP-48 (HP48), SUPER-CHIP modern and SUPER-CHIP HP48 differ in shifts, memory transfers and drawing. Use SUPER-CHIP modern for the complete programs in this guide. Their EXIT instruction is not part of classic CHIP-8.